= Electric current — Technical =

== Rate and flux ==
For charge crossing an oriented surface, the signed current is the rate of net charge transfer:

<pre>
I = dQ / dt
</pre>

In a continuous-current-density model:

<pre>
I = integral over surface (J · dA)
</pre>

Here J is electric current density and dA is an oriented surface element. The orientation determines the sign. The SI unit is A = C/s.

== Charge carriers and macroscopic modeling ==
For a simple uniform conductor with one mobile carrier type, one common drift approximation is I = n q A v_d, where n is number density, q the signed carrier charge, A the cross-sectional area and v_d signed drift speed along the chosen direction. Complex materials and ionic systems may require multiple species and nonuniform fields.

Under a steady-state, lumped, ohmic approximation, V = IR. Capacitors, nonlinear devices and time-varying fields require more general circuit and electromagnetic analysis.

== Limitations ==
Carrier drift speed is not the same as electromagnetic signal-propagation speed. A current measurement needs a specified surface or circuit branch, a defined time interval or bandwidth and a suitable instrument.

